The paper used an in silico prediction followed by RT-PCR and sequencing validation to identify honeybee bursicon cystine knot proteins. After an earlier model proposing a single fusion open reading frame for bursicon and its subunit was not supported by RT-PCR, the authors reanalysed the honeybee genomic locus using FGENESH and predicted two separate cystine knot protein ORFs. Bursicon-specific primers were then designed, and the corresponding ORFs were amplified from cDNA synthesized from honeybee larval total RNA. The cDNA was generated from total RNA isolated with a lipid-tissue RNA purification kit, treated on-column with DNase to remove genomic DNA, reverse transcribed with M-MLV reverse transcriptase using random hexamer priming at 37°C, and then treated with RNase H to remove RNA. PCR products were cloned into pCR2.1 TOPO and verified by DNA sequencing. This approach is relevant to the proposed C. chinensis research plan because it demonstrates how bioinformatically predicted Bursicon subunit candidates, such as CcBurs-α and CcBurs-β, can be experimentally validated by cDNA amplification and sequencing, and it supports the broader hypothesis that Bursicon functions as a heterodimer rather than a single multidomain protein. Although the paper does not address the receptor, the same validation logic can be applied to CcBurs-R.
[results]
The RT-PCR and sequencing workflow recovered two distinct ORFs corresponding to bursicon and its subunit cystine knot protein. Both cloned ORFs contained a predicted secretory signal sequence and were in full agreement with the in silico predicted ORFs. The result rejected the earlier single-ORF fusion-protein model and supported a heterodimeric Bursicon organization in honeybee. No quantitative expression values, statistical comparisons, or functional assays were reported in the provided sections.
