- venue: Frontiers in Insect Science
- citation count: 10
[method]
The paper used a temperature-exposure rearing method for newly hatched first instar spotted lanternfly nymphs. Egg masses were held at constant 15°C or a fluctuating Napa-like regime, hatched nymphs were placed in mesh cages with grape and tree-of-heaven plants, and first instars were exposed to constant 15°C or an alternating 20/5°C regime consisting of 16 hours at 20°C and 8 hours at 5°C. Instar duration was measured from hatch to second instar molt, with molts treated as censored observations and unnatural deaths excluded. Survival and duration were analyzed using Kaplan-Meier product-limit estimates and gamma-distribution mixed models, with population included as a random effect. This method relates to the proposed research plan because it tests temperature responsiveness in newly hatched first instar nymphs, but it measures developmental duration rather than Bursicon gene expression, CcTRPM activity, miRNA regulation, or seasonal polyphenism.
[results]
First instar duration was significantly longer by 6 days under the 20/5°C alternating low-temperature treatment than under constant 15°C (F = 21.08, df = 1, 15, p < 0.0001). This indicates that low-temperature exposure can prolong early nymphal development in this system, providing a phenotypic analogue for the proposed 25°C versus 10°C Bursicon expression time course, although no gene-expression or cuticle-pigmentation outcomes were reported.
[context]
The study used egg masses from New Jersey, Pennsylvania, and Virginia populations; Pennsylvania populations were combined for most nymphal data. Forty-five egg masses each from New Jersey and Pennsylvania were placed in the fluctuating regime, while the remainder were held at 15°C. Rearing cages were maintained mostly at 25°C, 65% relative humidity, and a 14:10 light:dark cycle, with some cages at 20-22°C due to space limitations. First instars were reared in groups of 50-100 in small cages or 300-500 in large cages, with fresh host plants added weekly and daily removal of molts and dead individuals. The experimental design included population-level replication and statistical adjustment for population variation, but it did not include molecular assays, RNAi, qRT-PCR, or seasonal-form classification, so its direct applicability to the proposed Bursicon mechanism is limited to temperature-exposure and early-instar developmental context.
