Processing method: To test the central hypothesis that Bursicon signaling mediates seasonal polyphenism in C. chinensis: We will conduct RNAi experiments by feeding newly hatched 1st instar nymphs with dsRNAs targeting CcBurs-α, CcBurs-β, CcBurs-R, or control dsEGFP. We will assess RNAi efficiency through qRT-PCR and evaluate functional outcomes through cuticle pigment extraction, chitin content determination, transmission electron microscopy for cuticle thickness measurement, and morphological transition percentage analysis.
Processing method: To test the proposed post-transcriptional regulation of Bursicon signaling by miRNAs: We will predict miRNAs targeting CcBurs-R using bioinformatics tools (miRanda and Targetscan), then validate target relationships through dual-luciferase reporter assays, RNA immunoprecipitation (RIP) assays, and fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH). We will also conduct functional studies using agomir and antagomir treatments.
Processing method: To link the known low-temperature cue to Bursicon regulation: We will expose newly hatched 1st instar nymphs of summer-form to different temperature treatments (25°C vs 10°C) and measure the expression of Bursicon genes at 3, 6, and 10 days post-treatment to determine temperature responsiveness.
Processing method: To demonstrate the structural basis of Bursicon signaling: We will express individual Bursicon subunits and co-express them in HEK293T cells, then use SDS-PAGE under reduced and non-reduced conditions to demonstrate homodimer and heterodimer formation.
Processing method: To connect Bursicon signaling to cuticle remodeling: We will examine the expression of chitin biosynthesis pathway genes (CcTre1 and CcCHS1) following Bursicon signaling manipulation to establish the downstream molecular cascade.
Processing method: To define baseline seasonal expression differences: We will analyze temporal and spatial expression patterns of CcBurs-α, CcBurs-β, and CcBurs-R across different developmental stages and tissues using qRT-PCR. We will compare expression levels between summer-form and winter-form individuals to establish baseline differences.
