[method]
The paper used inferential statistics to compare gas exchange, ion, osmolality, and carbon-to-nitrogen data across leaf age, rootstock variety, salinity treatment, and diurnal time. One-way ANOVA tested leaf-age effects within each variety-treatment combination; two-way ANOVA tested leaf-age by treatment effects within each time point or variety and variety-treatment effects within each leaf age; Tukey HSD was used for post hoc pairwise comparisons; Student's t-test was used for specific pairwise comparisons between treatments within a variety-leaf age combination or between varieties. Analyses were performed in R 4.2.1. This directly relates to the proposed Bursicon research plan, which also specifies Student's t-test for pairwise comparisons and ANOVA followed by Tukey's HSD for multiple comparisons, but the paper applies the same logic to a plant physiology factorial design rather than insect molecular treatments.
[results]
Using these tests, photosynthesis was significantly correlated with leaf age in both grapevine rootstocks (P < 0.01), with maximum photosynthesis at 28-35 days followed by decline. Between 28 and 42 days, photosynthesis declined by an average of 16.7% and PSII efficiency declined by 13.85%. Under control conditions, SO4 had 28% higher daily intrinsic water-use efficiency than 1103P. Two-way ANOVA was used for midday osmolality; the authors concluded there were no significant variety differences, although the text also states P < 0.05, making that specific statistical statement internally inconsistent. From a 6 am baseline, age-associated decline in stomatal conductance was 57% in 1103P and 65% in SO4, equivalent to about 1.5% lower conductance potential per day of leaf age. In 1103P, old leaves had 32% lower stomatal conductance and 37% lower photosynthesis than young leaves, while 49-day-old leaves still retained about 60% of maximum photosynthesis. For ion content, comparing only 21-day-old leaves suggested SO4 excluded sodium better than 1103P, but leaves 14 days older showed no variety difference.
[context]
The statistical comparisons were made on data from a net-house pot experiment with two grapevine rootstocks, 1103P and SO4, using 4-week-old cuttings planted in 20-L sand-filled pots in 3 blocks with 9 replicates per rootstock-treatment combination. Plants received drip irrigation with a 33% leaching fraction and 16.7% Hoagland solution supplying 40 ppm nitrogen; salinity treatments were 0.7 dS m-1 control and 4 dS m-1 with 30 mM NaCl, applied from June 3 to August 10, 2020. Gas exchange was measured with an LI-6800 system; midday weekly measurements used 400 ppm CO2, 1000 umol m-2 s-1 PFD, 35 C, and 50% RH, while diurnal measurements on 21- and 63-day-old leaves were taken at 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, and 18 h with chamber settings matched to hourly conditions. Ion, elemental, and osmolality measurements were made from marked leaves, including chloride by silver ion titration, sodium by ICP-OES after 0.5 M nitric acid extraction, carbon and nitrogen by elemental analysis, and osmolality by vapor pressure osmometry from frozen leaf sap. For the proposed insect research plan, the relevant transferable element is the statistical framework for comparing treatment, control, and multi-factor groups, not the plant physiological measurements themselves.
[end related work 1]

Processing method: To support quantitative comparisons across treatments: Statistical analysis will be performed using Student's t-test for pairwise comparisons and ANOVA followed by Tukey's HSD for multiple comparisons.
Processing method: To establish the candidate Bursicon genes in C. chinensis: We will identify and characterize CcBurs-α and CcBurs-β as the two subunits of Bursicon in C. chinensis, along with their receptor CcBurs-R. We will use bioinformatics analysis, sequence alignment, phylogenetic analysis, and protein structure prediction to validate these genes.
