  "support": "The strongest support is that [(Lupo et al., 2023-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/70880cfac4fe9481b6f58c3e86bbce22754aa7d5) used the same inferential-statistics logic proposed for the Bursicon plan: Student's t-test for specific pairwise comparisons and ANOVA followed by Tukey HSD for multiple comparisons. In that study, one-way ANOVA tested leaf-age effects within each variety-treatment combination, two-way ANOVA tested leaf-age by treatment and variety-treatment effects, and Tukey HSD was used for post hoc pairwise comparisons in R 4.2.1. The experiment had two grapevine rootstocks, 1103P and SO4, 9 replicates per rootstock-treatment combination, 3 blocks, 20-L sand-filled pots, 33% leaching fraction, 16.7% Hoagland solution with 40 ppm nitrogen, and salinity treatments of 0.7 dS m-1 control and 4 dS m-1 with 30 mM NaCl. Using this framework, the authors detected meaningful quantitative differences, including photosynthesis significantly correlated with leaf age in both rootstocks (P < 0.01), a 16.7% photosynthesis decline and 13.85% PSII efficiency decline between 28 and 42 days, 28% higher daily intrinsic water-use efficiency in SO4 under control, 57% and 65% age-associated stomatal conductance declines in 1103P and SO4, and 32% lower stomatal conductance and 37% lower photosynthesis in old versus young 1103P leaves. This directly supports the proposed use of t-tests and ANOVA/Tukey for comparing treatment, control, and multi-factor groups in the Bursicon research plan. A second, slightly weaker support is that the related work's factorial structure—variety, treatment, leaf age, and diurnal time—maps onto the proposed insect design, which also has multiple fixed factors such as 25 C vs 10 C, 3/6/10 days post-treatment, RNAi targets CcBurs-alpha, CcBurs-beta, CcBurs-R, and dsEGFP, and summer-form versus winter-form comparisons. A third, weaker support is implementation detail: the related work specified R 4.2.1 and standard post hoc testing, which supports the proposed plan's need to specify software and statistical procedures, although the plant gas-exchange, ion, and osmolality measurements themselves are not directly transferable to insect molecular assays.",
  "contradictions": "The strongest contradiction is that [(Lupo et al., 2023-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/70880cfac4fe9481b6f58c3e86bbce22754aa7d5) contains an internally inconsistent statistical statement: two-way ANOVA for midday osmolality was reported as showing no significant variety differences while the text also states P < 0.05. This warns that the proposed method's generic phrase, Student's t-test for pairwise comparisons and ANOVA followed by Tukey's HSD for multiple comparisons, is insufficient unless the Bursicon plan pre-specifies hypotheses, comparison families, exact P values, and consistency checks; otherwise conclusions may contradict reported statistics. A second contradiction is replication and power: the related work used 9 replicates per rootstock-treatment combination and 3 blocks, whereas the proposed plan states n=3 with at least 50 individuals per replicate. For the proposed multi-factor insect experiments—temperature by time, RNAi target by form, rescue, miRNA agomir/antagomir, and downstream CcTre1/CcCHS1 expression—n=3 biological replicates is likely underpowered for interaction effects and multiple endpoints, especially if the 50 individuals are pooled or treated as technical replicates rather than independent biological units. A third contradiction is endpoint mismatch: the proposed plan implies t-test/ANOVA for all quantitative comparisons, but transition percentage from summer-form to winter-form is a proportion/binomial outcome, qRT-PCR expression often requires log2 fold-change and reference-gene validation, TEM cuticle thickness may involve repeated measurements per specimen, and dual-luciferase, RIP, FISH, agomir, and antagomir assays may require different models. The related work used t-tests only for specific pairwise comparisons within a defined variety-leaf age combination or between varieties, not as a default for all outcomes. A fourth, weaker contradiction is that the related work's plant physiological context—LI-6800 gas exchange at 400 ppm CO2, 1000 umol m-2 s-1 PFD, 35 C, and 50% RH, ICP-OES sodium after 0.5 M nitric acid extraction, elemental analysis, and vapor pressure osmometry—does not directly validate the insect molecular assay setup, so its support is limited to the statistical framework rather than the biological measurements.",
  "suggested_action": "Most important, replace the generic statistical statement with endpoint-specific, pre-registered models: define the biological unit (individual nymph, replicate pool, TEM section, or assay well), randomization/blocking, fixed effects (temperature, time, RNAi target, form, treatment), interactions, and random/repeated effects; use two-way or mixed ANOVA/linear mixed models for continuous endpoints, binomial/logistic regression or chi-square/Fisher exact tests for transition percentage, and appropriate models for qRT-PCR (delta-delta Ct, log2 fold change, reference gene validation), TEM (nested/repeated measures), and miRNA assays. Second, increase or justify replication and conduct a power analysis: the related work's 9 replicates per treatment combination and 3 blocks is a useful benchmark, and the proposed n=3 should be expanded or the 50 individuals per replicate analyzed as independent biological units if justified; report expected effect sizes based on comparable biological differences, such as the 16.7% photosynthesis decline, 28% water-use efficiency difference, and 32-37% leaf-age differences reported in [(Lupo et al., 2023-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/70880cfac4fe9481b6f58c3e86bbce22754aa7d5), and set alpha, power, and minimum detectable effect. Third, specify multiple-comparison control: use Tukey HSD only for planned all-pairwise comparisons within a factor, use Bonferroni/FDR for multiple endpoints or genes, and reserve Student's t-test for a small number of pre-specified pairwise control-versus-treatment comparisons with correction. Fourth, add assumption and reporting safeguards: test normality/homoscedasticity, apply transformations where needed, report exact P values, effect sizes, confidence intervals, software (e.g., R version and packages), and ensure conclusions match P values to avoid the inconsistency observed in [(Lupo et al., 2023-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/70880cfac4fe9481b6f58c3e86bbce22754aa7d5). Fifth, include baseline and control details: compare against dsEGFP and untreated summer/winter forms, report RNAi efficiency before functional endpoints, and for rescue and miRNA studies include specificity and dose controls.",
